WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.802
The Black Death was one of the most

00:00:05.802 --> 00:00:08.970
devastating pandemics in human history.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:13.970
Between 1347 and 1351,
it wiped out 30% to 50%

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.790
of the entire population of Europe.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:18.980
And if there's one animal to blame,

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:22.250
it's likely infected fleas,

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:25.653
making them one of the
deadliest insects in history.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.793
Now, of course, fleas no
longer kill millions of people.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:34.090
But as it turns out, they
still transmit plague,

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.848
they're still a nuisance,

00:00:35.848 --> 00:00:38.985
and they're still incredibly
difficult to kill.

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:46.260
Fleas are one of the
oldest pests on the planet.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.545
In fact, primitive fleas
were dining on dinosaur blood

00:00:49.545 --> 00:00:52.800
about 165 million years ago.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.610
And they're also one of the most abundant.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:58.420
Today, there are more than 2,500 species

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.860
of fleas across the
world, like the cat flea.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.820
Those are the ones you're
most likely to find

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.003
feasting on your dog or your cat.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:10.590
And then there are species
like the human flea,

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.580
which actually seek out
human hosts specifically.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.780
And seeking is a flea's specialty.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:20.050
They can sense your breath, your warmth,

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.670
and even the vibrations of your feet.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:27.403
And when they find you,
they do what fleas do best:

00:01:27.403 --> 00:01:28.940
jump.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.340
According to one study,
some fleas can jump

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.630
nearly 100 times farther than
the length of their body,

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:35.920
which would be like an average man

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.170
jumping nearly 175 meters into the air.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.620
The secret to their spring
is a stretchy protein

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.490
in their legs called resilin,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.551
and it works like a rubber
band in a slingshot.

00:01:47.551 --> 00:01:49.534
Elizabeth Warburton: They
have kind of a hook-and-latch

00:01:49.534 --> 00:01:50.509
type of system.

00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:53.280
And when the flea lets go of
the latch, they just jump.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.440
Narrator: Now, that makes
fleas difficult to capture

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.363
and kill, but it's what
they do once they land

00:01:58.363 --> 00:02:01.360
that makes them a truly terrifying pest.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.510
First, they use blades in their mouth

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:07.420
called maxillary laciniae
to tear into your flesh.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.540
Then they use a straw-like structure

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:12.390
called an epipharynx to slurp it all up.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.720
It's not unlike...

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:16.510
Warburton: If you spilled
your drink on a table

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.539
and you took a straw and
you tried to drink it up.

00:02:18.539 --> 00:02:19.690
[slurping]

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:21.310
Narrator: Yum.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.990
Adding insult to injury,
fleas have proteins

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:26.270
in their saliva that
keep your blood flowing

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:29.130
so they have a constant supply of food.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:32.140
And that's actually why they itch so much.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.623
Your body (or your pet's) is
reacting to those proteins.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.930
But there's one flea that takes feeding

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:41.460
to another, more horrifying level.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:45.370
And, unfortunately, it
prefers to prey on humans:

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:49.150
Tunga penetrans, aka the chigoe flea.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.030
Instead of drinking blood
from the surface of your skin,

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.900
the females burrow into you,

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.540
and then they start laying eggs.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.120
Warburton: Most of her
is buried into the skin,

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.080
but kind of the back part,
where she's releasing eggs,

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.270
is exposed.

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.830
Narrator: And fleas lay a lot of eggs.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:11.320
Up to 40 each day and as many
as 2,000 in their lifetimes.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:14.330
So if your dog brings back
a few fleas from the park,

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.510
you could soon have thousands.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:20.030
And the truth is they can do a
lot worse than make you itch.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:23.010
You see, even modern fleas
can transfer diseases

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:27.380
like typhus, bartonellosis,
and, yes, plague.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.900
In fact, in the summer of 2019,

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:31.970
the discovery of prairie dogs infected

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.860
by plague-ridden fleas shut
down parts of a Denver suburb.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:38.330
And in 2015, a teen reportedly died

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:41.630
from contact with fleas
infected with bubonic plague.

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:45.230
Now, fortunately, fatal
fleas are pretty rare,

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:48.230
and most likely it'll
be your pets, not you,

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:49.900
that have to deal with them.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:53.370
But if you do see one
or two jumping around,

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.910
you definitely should
try to get rid of them.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:56.883
And here's how.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:01.840
First, identify the source.
And if it's your pet,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:05.710
treat them with an insect growth
regulator, like Lufenuron.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:09.010
Next, clean your home, though
you might want to steer clear

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:12.080
of flea bombs or foggers;
they're inconvenient

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.960
and don't always reach
the nooks and crannies

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:15.750
where fleas are hanging out.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.620
Instead, repeatedly
vacuum or mop your floors

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:21.070
and toss any bedding
into the washing machine,

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:23.610
making sure to dry it at high heat.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:27.110
After that, all you can do is sit, wait,

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:30.248
and hope that you're flea-free.

